WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:10.990
Malcolm Grant: Moving on to item 12, which
is the healthy workforce update. Ian?

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:17.200
Ian Dodge: Thank you, Malcolm. I will touch
on the NHS Healthy Workplace programme, before

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:24.419
I turn to Karen on the plans for NHS England’s
own staff. We have four main strands of work

00:00:24.419 --> 00:00:29.930
here. The first is action on healthy food,
including trialling in four hospitals an NHS

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:37.090
sugar tax. Secondly, as part of the GP Forward
View and the work led by Arvind and Ros, a

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:46.780
new primary care offer. Thirdly, we are working
with 12 demonstrator NHS employers. It is

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:54.339
early days, but the plans have been agreed,
and we are seeing some encouraging local progress.

00:00:54.339 --> 00:01:01.890
For example, in April Rotherham CCG achieved
an excellent rating in every category of the

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:10.630
Workplace Wellbeing Charter, and West Midlands
Ambulance Service have helped 248 staff lose

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:19.579
almost 40 pounds per person. The real challenge,
then, is clearly: how do we get spread once

00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:25.670
the 12 demonstrators have completed their
actions? That is where the fourth element

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:31.130
comes in, which is the National CQUIN Scheme,
which is incentivising all employers to make

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:33.850
progress in this space.
Malcolm Grant: 40 pounds?

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:37.009
Ian Dodge: 40, four-zero.
Michelle Mitchell: That is a lot!

00:01:37.009 --> 00:01:41.119
David Roberts: Pounds per person?
Ian Dodge: So I understand. I have not actually

00:01:41.119 --> 00:01:58.350
been to the West Midlands Ambulance Service.
I thought you would like that fact.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:04.310
Malcolm Grant: We are delighted. May we move
quickly to Karen?

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:11.340
Karen Wheeler: I just wanted to say, of course,
NHS England is strongly championing this,

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:17.490
and indeed has, I think, led the way on some
of the thinking in our own organisation since

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:25.340
2013. We kicked that off by trying to think
about what exactly were the health issues

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:32.600
affecting our own staff, and two key themes
that came out of that were mental health;

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:40.870
we actually had significant numbers of people
off on sick leave due to stress, so mental

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:45.070
health has been a very strong theme of the
work that we are doing inside NHS England,

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:53.540
and particularly we have a large number of
people now trained up to provide help to their

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:57.820
colleagues in the organisation, and that has
been of huge benefit.

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:08.900
The other area is of physical activity, so
just the amount of absence due to unhealthiness

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:16.500
due to lack of physical activity is a concern,
and therefore we are stepping up all of that;

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:23.090
the support that we can give to employees
to improve their physical activity is a priority.

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:26.330
I was not going to go through the detail of
what is in the report, but I just think it

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:31.060
is worth noting the progress we are making
inside NHS England.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:35.800
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much. I think
we are assured by the progress, and it is

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:43.010
also something which we will come back to
at the HR strategy and remuneration committee.

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:49.320
Thank you very much.
We then move on to the reports from the Board committees.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:56.940
David Roberts: There will be a proper written
report for the next Board, but I think given

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:05.500
the timing, just to repeat: the Audit Committee
looked at the progress from both an internal

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:11.950
audit perspective and external audit, and
is comfortable and assured that everything

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:23.030
is on track for our annual report. The second
was that we looked extensively at fraud strategy,

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:29.629
and I think felt good progress, but a need
to really concentrate on a few things and

00:04:29.629 --> 00:04:39.710
make it demonstrable. The third thing was
that we looked at the governance process,

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:47.430
and a number of the products of that process,
where there has been material progress in

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:56.610
the last 12 months, and that is reflected
in some of the opinions from internal audit,

00:04:56.610 --> 00:05:01.569
although the embedding is the hard bit which
is yet to come. Those would be the three things.

00:05:01.569 --> 00:05:06.949
I am happy to take any questions.
Malcolm Grant: Are there any questions for

00:05:06.949 --> 00:05:09.729
David? No? Good, thank you.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:17.300
David Roberts: I would propose just to respond
to any questions on the paper, as set out.

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:19.219
We have covered most of them.
Malcolm Grant: Many members of the Board are

00:05:19.219 --> 00:05:27.009
on the Commissioning Committee, so I think
we are pretty well informed.

00:05:27.009 --> 00:05:33.960
Noel Gordon: I think items one to six in the
documented summary, Malcolm, have been included

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:40.280
already in our discussion with Jonathan, and
items seven to eight in Paul’s discussion

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:46.080
on finances. I would say very little on item
nine, other than to reiterate really what

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:51.370
it says: the Specialised Commissioning Committee
is going to meet on Tuesday, and it will by

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:57.749
that time have received legal advice on whether
it does or does not have the powers to commission

00:05:57.749 --> 00:06:05.639
PrEP, which is the antiviral drug for HIV.
That process is in play as we speak, and we

00:06:05.639 --> 00:06:09.729
will meet on Tuesday to review it, discuss
it and make our decision

00:06:09.729 --> 00:06:14.650
Malcolm Grant: Okay, well thank you for the
update on that. Any questions, comments, on

00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:16.560
Specialised Commissioning? No.

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:23.680
Moira Gibb: I do not think there is anything
I want to add to the report.

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:30.610
Malcolm Grant: Any comments or queries raised
on the report? No?

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:35.139
I think that brings us to the end of the agenda.
I do not have any other business. Does any

00:06:35.139 --> 00:06:39.499
member of the Board have any other business
to bring to the attention to the Board? No?

00:06:39.499 --> 00:06:44.060
In which case, may I invite the Board to pass
the usual resolution about moving into private

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:50.479
session for the business of the private Board?
Thank you very much, and I declare the meeting

00:06:50.479 --> 00:06:51.319
to be closed. Thank you.

